WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:02.563
- [Interviewer] All right.

00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:04.373
So Rice, Rice, where'd you grow up?

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.720
- I grew up mostly in what
they call Napper Branch

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.709
which is located in Beach Fort Kentucky.

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:13.370
- [Interviewer] And this is Leslie County?

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.400
- [Rice] Yes.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.130
- [Interviewer] Tell me
about your childhood.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.880
You had both your parents growing up?

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.810
- Yeah, we was all family.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.170
- [Interviewer] How, Mom and Dad?

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.550
Good childhood?

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.660
- [Rice] Yeah, we had,

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.010
- [Interviewer] What kind of stuff?

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.440
I mean, you grew up, you know

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.000
it's a very poor part of the country.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.430
Would you, would you consider your family,

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.750
- We were very poor.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.940
- [Interviewer] Yeah. You had electricity?

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.120
You had running water?

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.410
- We had electricity, but we
didn't have running water.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.073
We didn't have indoor plumbing.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.320
We had, what's known as
an outhouse of coercion.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.903
We had a well, we had a
garden, we had simple things.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.113
- [Interviewer] What kind
of work did your father do?

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.790
- Basically, I remember him working some

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.560
for the state at one point.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.670
Didn't seem like a very long time.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:11.320
And what's ironic about it,
my daddy, well, he's older now

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.320
but he drunk a lot and I
didn't know who he was.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.990
I didn't. I didn't really
know who he was 'til

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.743
I was like four or five year old.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.360
You know, it's just
something that I guess where

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.340
I was with my mother.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.984
I was a mommy's baby.

00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:31.817
[Laughs]

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.673
And I was with my mother
the most of the time.

00:01:36.599 --> 00:01:37.450
- [Interviewer] And you got
a high school education?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.940
- Yes.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.920
- [Interviewer] What kind
of work have you done?

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.403
- I've been a timber cutter.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.930
Straight, that's what
I do. Timber cutting.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.780
- [Interviewer] Which is
the only real alternative

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.960
to coal mining for most men, right?

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.093
- Well, I just was afraid of coal mines.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.050
I'll just be honest.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.520
I didn't like the being underground.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.223
I didn't like the thoughts of it.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.880
You know? It was just something.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.383
I felt free out in the woods.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.800
And another reason that
I took on timber cutting,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.033
I had to work.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:17.500
I had to work.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.623
So what do you do?

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.580
Somebody offers you a timber cutting job.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.913
Pick up a saw and try it.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.503
It wasn't easy.

00:02:28.156 --> 00:02:30.360
It never was easy, but it
got easier as time went on.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.270
Even though I didn't know nothing.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.290
I didn't know nothing about
my timber cutting, you know

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.420
and I've had some very bad
experiences, timber cutting.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.253
- [Interviewer] What kind of stuff?

00:02:41.253 --> 00:02:44.010
It's a dangerous job, just
like coal mining is too.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.020
- Yeah.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.610
Yeah, they say it's dangerous, you know,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.053
And I can only speak for
tiller cutting because it's all

00:02:49.053 --> 00:02:50.786
I've ever done.

00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:52.950
- [Interviewer] What kind
of stuff have you seen?

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.240
- Do what?

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.190
- [Interviewer] When
you say that you've seen

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.180
dangerous things, what kind
of stuff have you seen?

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.013
- Timber cutting.

00:02:59.013 --> 00:03:00.220
- [Interviewer] Logging. Yeah.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.463
- I've been hurt twice pretty bad.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.473
I got hit in the face really
bad once and knocked out.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.563
Knocked to bled to death on the spot.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.400
And then I got hurt later years

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.730
which really put me out
of the timber cutting.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.410
When there was a tree pushed down on me

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.310
and busted my hard hat, cut
my head, busted all my disc

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.873
in my neck and five in my lower back.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.940
And I've had an operation on my back

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.940
but my neck is not, it can't be repaired.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.430
- [Interviewer] But you're
still working today.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.530
I saw you this Sunday morning

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:46.510
and you were out there working.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:48.170
- Well I wasn't doing a lot.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.713
Still, I wasn't doing no.

00:03:50.713 --> 00:03:53.890
See there's a lot difference
in me now than then.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.772
- [Interviewer] Yeah, but
you're planting potatoes.

00:03:55.772 --> 00:03:56.732
- What now?

00:03:56.732 --> 00:03:58.430
- [Interviewer] You were
planting potatoes this morning?

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.593
- I was digging them up.

00:03:59.593 --> 00:04:00.610
- [Interviewer] Oh, digging them up.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.080
- Yeah, I was digging them up.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.130
It's fall.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.310
- [Interviewer] Yeah. It
shows you I'm a city slicker.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:07.710
- Yeah. It was fall.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.182
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled?

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:10.015
- What?

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:10.950
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled?

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.670
- Not much. Not, very little.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.870
Very little. Biggest
'venture I've ever been

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.500
on was my 8th grade school trip

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.293
which was to Mammoth Cave in,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.470
down toward Elizabethtown, I guess,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.670
somewhere down that way.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.930
- [Interviewer] But
you're very happy here?

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:29.780
- Oh yeah.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.523
- [Interviewer] It's a
beautiful part of the country.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.620
- I wouldn't know no other life of course.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:36.453
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.623
- 53 years in the woods.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.203
What else do you know?

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.910
But you know I've found it interesting,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.863
you know, and I know like I said,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.940
I don't like being belittled whatsoever.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:56.550
When I went to school

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.430
I could sit in school and
listen, enough to pass classes.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.560
I could make the grades I wanted to make.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.480
If I wanted to just pass a
class, I could just pass it.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.730
If I wanted to make straight
A's I can make straight A's.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.143
And it was just all in
what I wanted to do.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.853
I never found it to be
a complicated thing.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:18.693
I never did.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:23.390
- [Interviewer] What are your
favorite things about living

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.620
in Appalachia and what are the
hardest parts of living here?

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.740
Because it's not an easy life.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.510
- It's not easy, but

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.800
well, to me, it's just life and it just,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.450
what you would consider horrible may not,

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.720
even to me be more than just day to day.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.083
I mean, it might not be that way for me.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.540
You know, I've been men in lifetimes.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.000
Growing up, I packed coal.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.090
I packed water.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.493
I was in the snow.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.780
There was times that
I'd get up of a morning.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.800
And what bucket water
was froze, fire was out.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.970
Snow would blow in the house.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.340
We had that.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.880
We had a lot of that.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:12.100
And, you know, even my

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.460
my family today has got is so much better

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.730
even though we ain't got it good either.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.480
I mean, what you would consider good.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.030
But to us it is good.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.620
And we're very happy with what we have.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:26.563
It's all on being thankful for
what you do have, you know?

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.420
And I think God did, I was
born here in the Appalachians.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.590
He give me a whole, I've
got a whole experience

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.993
that millions of people will never have.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.350
If you understand what I'm saying.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:42.183
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:06:42.183 --> 00:06:43.720
Do you feel like you've lived
the life that you wanted to?

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:44.900
- Yeah, pretty much.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:48.230
You know, I've done things I
shouldn't and I regret things,

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:51.200
some things, and I won't mention them

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.270
because it's no need
to because they're gone

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:55.620
and ain't no changing them.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.510
But yeah, I do.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.677
I do really enjoy my life.

00:07:00.677 --> 00:07:02.880
And I enjoy living in Appalachia

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.840
and I'm proud of Appalachian people.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.740
- [Interviewer] Yeah. What is
it about the Appalachian is

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.370
that makes them so unique because they

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.340
they seem to be so willing
to help their neighbor.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.669
They seem to be so kind.

00:07:12.669 --> 00:07:15.150
- It's the teaching.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.150
It's the teaching of loving,
Godliness, Christianity.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:23.650
Who was our parents and our grandparents?

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.450
They went to church, they believed in

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:29.870
and they would correct you
when you was doing wrong.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.253
They instilled it in you as a child.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.537
They did.

00:07:34.537 --> 00:07:35.370
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.738
- You understand that?

00:07:36.738 --> 00:07:38.210
- [Interviewer] And they
were great role models too.

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:39.043
- Yeah.

00:07:39.043 --> 00:07:43.520
That makes, and not just that, but when

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.440
you have experienced these things

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.030
and I'm talking about personally
experienced the things

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:53.480
and the things that you
can't see for instance

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.993
and then it gives you a
whole outlook on life.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.530
If you understand what I'm
saying, then it gives you

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:03.860
a whole different.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.500
And then in the end,
you know, we all think

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.570
about being rich.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.023
And I have been guilty of it too.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:16.050
But when you look back,
would you trade what you have

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.700
for all the wealth in the world?

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.520
No, I would not.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.100
I would not trade it.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.050
I would not try trade
everything I've experienced

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.443
and have for all the wealth in the world.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.283
Because money couldn't
value what I've lived.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:36.430
even though it's been hard at
times, I've been, like I said

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:41.030
I've been out, I've cut
timber and snow drifted up

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:45.240
to my waist and work week in and week out,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.953
week in and week out for my family.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.220
And that was, you know,
that was something to do

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.013
for, is your family.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.230
And when you can't give
that kind of effort

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.283
for your family, you don't
know what you've missed.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.350
- [Interviewer] Growing up
here in, in Eastern Kentucky,

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:08.750
What's your favorite memory?

00:09:10.104 --> 00:09:14.050
- [Rice] Hmm. You know,
my favorite memory,

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:19.050
that would be, I have memories
of my little Fox that I had

00:09:19.793 --> 00:09:21.100
- [Interviewer] You had a pet fox?

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.410
- [Rice] and I still miss it.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.243
I still miss.

00:09:23.243 --> 00:09:25.610
- [Interviewer] It is
a pet Fox that you...

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.230
- [Rice] I raised her from this little,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:30.580
well, my daughter and my
granddaughter and my wife

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:32.820
they really, I'll give them credit

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.660
because they's the ones
that set up with it

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.040
and seen to it, you know,

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:39.403
a lot of times when it was very small.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.693
I was the one found it.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:48.770
But I remember a dog that
I had when I was a kid.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:55.624
And he was my friend
because my sister was blind.

00:09:55.624 --> 00:09:58.100
And my brother, he was, he was retarded

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.390
to some extent in, you know,
he couldn't be out with me.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.450
I didn't have neighbors, and I

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:06.410
didn't have nobody to
play with, but my doll

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:10.880
and I was out, I was out
walking in the hills.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:12.460
My dog was with me all the time.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.963
He was my best friend,
you know, and the woods.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.520
And I always tried everything that I saw

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.010
out in the woods to see if it tasted good.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.800
If it tasted good, I would eat it.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.500
I knew blueberries or huckleberries.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:32.350
We call them huckleberries.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:33.550
I would eat them, but I

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.900
didn't know what they
was for a very long time.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:39.310
I would eat planting salad.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.770
I didn't know what it was for a long time.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:44.350
A lot of the things that grow out there

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:47.770
but they were some things
that I was backed up.

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:50.130
(laughs)

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.780
Well, some of them, you
know, it's just some of them.

00:10:54.019 --> 00:10:57.180
- [Interviewer] So your Fox,
it behaved like a pet dog, or?

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.620
- She was more like a cat.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.593
She was really quiet and
she was extremely smart.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.140
You could only, you could take the Fox

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.053
and teach it one time.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:12.260
And then it would mind you on command.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.820
You could say, "come
here", she would come here.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.740
You would tell her not
to bother something.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:22.400
And you told her not to,
she would not bother it.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.933
If you understand what
I'm saying, she was, well

00:11:24.933 --> 00:11:28.373
like I said about my fire, she
mind it better than children.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.420
Because once you taught her,

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.030
she did not stray away from that.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:35.270
- [Interviewer] And she
wasn't trying to get away.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:36.103
She was your pet.

00:11:36.103 --> 00:11:38.090
- No, she didn't want to get away.

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:41.853
She was given all the freedom
of outdoors as she wanted,

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.050
you know?

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:45.473
And she died in my lap.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.233
She died in my lap.

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.170
- [Interviewer] Did you
ever consider leaving?

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:54.615
- Consider leaving?

00:11:54.615 --> 00:11:55.760
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.050
- No.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.230
I will never consider
leaving left where I live.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:03.630
- [Interviewer] Do you know
anything about your grandparents

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.180
or your family?

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:09.203
- I remember my Grandma Bessie
which is Kyle's grandmother.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.660
Well, it'd be his
great-grandmother that's as far

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:16.200
as I remember grandparents.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.100
I do not remember my father's parents.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.140
- [Interviewer] But do you
know, like, how they survived?

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.060
What life was like for them?

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.933
- Well, Grandma just
lived, you know, simple.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:30.513
She was very simple.

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:34.720
You know, she was very simple.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.550
She worked hard. She raised two daughters.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:39.100
